    "narrative": "The analytics suggest a high-floor night for Ghost Ride the WHIP, headlined by **Trevor Story** (75.56) and **Vinnie Pasquantino** (72.92), who both benefit immensely from facing starting pitchers with xERAs north of 4.90. **Trevor Story** remains our most potent play, capitalizing on a +19% boost from the favorable matchup against Johnson, while **Vinnie Pasquantino** is locked into the heart of the order with a strong 72.92 efficiency score. **James Wood** also demands attention; despite facing Buehler, his elite .419 xwOBA and lead-off spot generate a 65.96 score, proving his value goes beyond simple park factors. We are also banking on **Mickey Moniak** (68.75) to exploit the favorable matchup against Warren, as his dynamic platoon advantage remains one of our most efficient sources of secondary production.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the card involved keeping **Cole Young** (49.60) on the bench. He hovered near the starting threshold, but ultimately, the depth of our middle infield and the relative stagnation of his projected order spot (8th) tipped the scales toward **Travis Bazzana** (61.46), who benefits from a significant 16% park factor bump. We are wary of the risks surrounding **Seiya Suzuki** (46.29), whose score plummeted due to a difficult matchup against Henderson; he is a marginal start today and will be on a short leash. Furthermore, while **Jose Caballero** remains a high-variance play due to his elite speed and potential for stolen bases, his 9th-order spot drag of -8% serves as a reminder that we are prioritizing efficiency to preserve our game caps during this final push.",
